                     <decoNote type="initial" subtype ="major">
                        <locus from="1r" facs="#GKS04-3275-00001r" to="1r">1r</locus> 
                        <p> Ornate initial ‘L’ with spiral interlacing. Colours: Red, blue. Below the title is a marginalia decoration in a spiral style </p>
                     </decoNote>
                     <decoNote type="initial" subtype ="major">
                        <locus from="2r" facs="#GKS04-3275-00002r" to="2r">2r</locus> 
                        <p> Ornate initial ‘M’ in a spiral flourish. This initial is one of the only instances where yellow ink is used in the manuscript. Colours: red, blue, yellow. </p>
                     </decoNote>
                     <decoNote type="initial" subtype ="major">
                        <locus from="70v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0070v" to="70v">70v</locus> 
                        <p> Ornate initial ‘E’ with a spiral flourish which ascends 2 lines, descends 3 lines, and extends into the left margin. A marginalia note is written between the spirals. The red ink has been imprinted on the facing leaf. Colours: red </p>
                     </decoNote>
                     <decoNote type="initial" subtype="minor">
                        <p> Minor initials are found throughout the manuscript in the same style as the major initials. From 6v onwards, minor initials appear in the left-hand margin of every paragraph, or law,  either spiraling leftwards or descending a line. A primary is example is <locus from="270v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0270v" to="286r">270v-286r</locus> which contains both chapter numerals and minor initials, ending with a spiraled border on <locus from="286r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0286r" to="286r">286r</locus>.</p>
                    </decoNote>
                    <decoNote type="border">
                        <p>At the ends of texts are spiraled borders in black ink which fill the margin:
                            <locus from="204v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0204v" to="204v">204v</locus>, 
                            <locus from="216r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0216r" to="216r">216r</locus>,
                            <locus from="289r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0289r" to="289r">289r</locus>,
                            <locus from="254r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0254r" to="254r">254r</locus>,
                            <locus from="269r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0269r" to="269r">269r</locus>,
                            <locus from="401v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0401v" to="401v">401v</locus>,
                            <locus from="436v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0436v" to="436v">436v</locus>, and
                            <locus from="447r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0447r" to="447r">447r</locus>.
                        </p>
                        <p>In some instances the text preceding the border descends in a triangular shape:
                            <list>
                                <item n="1"><locus from="269r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0269r" to="269r">269r</locus>: The text begins to decrease on line eleven from five words to two letter on line seventeen. </item>
                                <item n="2"><locus from="289r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0289r" to="289r">289r</locus>: Both paragraphs of text descends, the first due to lack of space on the line caused by the initial, but the second paragraph follows the triangular descending shape from four words to four letters on the final line. </item>
                                <item n="3"><locus from="292v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0292v" to="292v">292v</locus>: The text begins to descend on line fourteen, and the final two lines have the date ‘7 Dag July/ A 607. There is no spiraled border in the margin. </item>
                                <item n="4"><locus from="294v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0294v" to="294v">294v</locus>: The text descends on line one, from five words to a single word on line 4. There is no spiraled border. </item>
                                <item n="5"><locus from="320v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0320v" to="320v">320v</locus>: The text descends from the beginning of the chapter, from four words on line five, to a single word on line 16, before returning to a full length line on line sixteen.</item>
                                <item n="6"><locus from="436v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0436v" to="436v">436v</locus>:The text descends from lines nine to fifteen, from six words to one.</item>
                                <item n="7"><locus from="466v" facs="#GKS04-3275-0636v" to="466v">466v</locus>: The text descends from lines eleven to sixteen, from seven words to two. Below this is the date 1587 in roman numerals.</item>
                                <item n="8"><locus from="493r" facs="#GKS04-3275-0493r" to="493r">493r</locus>: The text decreases from lines thirteen to eighteen, from six words to one. The text ends with the date 1551 in roman numerals. </item>
                            </list>
                        </p>
                    </decoNote>
                </decoDesc>
                <additions>
                    <p>On the upper spine of the manuscript is written a variety of letters and symbols including ‘J’, presumably for Jónsbók, the date 1671, and an arrow pointing to the sign and various other letters.</p>
                </additions>
